PROJECT NAME:
NOVIN FORGE AI

PROJECT ID:
NOVIN-FORGE-AI

MISSION:
Build a serious, modular, security-first, AI-powered software engineering
workbench for Android development, ZIP project analysis, controlled code
integration, automated repair, testing, and continuous project evolution.

This is NOT a simple chatbot, ZIP viewer, code generator, or mock dashboard.

It must be a real engineering application with a visual interface, a
persistent project workspace, a backend processing engine, an AI reasoning
layer, security controls, a testing framework, version management,
approval workflows, and evidence-based reporting.

The primary purpose is to become the engineering environment used to
develop, repair, maintain, and eventually rebuild NOVIN NEXUS from its
existing project archives.

==================================================
1. MASTER ENGINEERING PRINCIPLES
==================================================

Follow these rules throughout implementation.

1. REALITY FIRST

Never claim that a file was repaired, code was executed, a build succeeded,
a vulnerability was fixed, or a test passed unless the corresponding
operation was actually performed and its result was recorded.

Distinguish clearly between:
- PASS
- PARTIAL
- FAIL
- BLOCKED
- UNKNOWN
- NOT TESTED
- NOT VERIFIED ON DEVICE

Every operation must have an execution status and supporting evidence.

2. PRESERVE THE SOURCE

Never overwrite an uploaded original archive.

Create an immutable source snapshot and calculate its SHA-256 hash.

All modifications must initially occur in an isolated working copy.

The original source must remain recoverable.

3. NO BLIND MERGING

Never merge files merely because their names are similar.

Never delete files merely because their hashes match.

Never automatically replace an existing implementation without
understanding its role, dependencies, callers, configuration, and tests.

4. EVIDENCE BEFORE CONFIDENCE

Separate verified facts, static-analysis findings, AI-generated hypotheses,
and unresolved questions.

The AI must not present an assumption as an observed fact.

5. HUMAN APPROVAL

Before modifying the main project, present:
- The proposed changes.
- The reasons for each change.
- The files affected.
- The expected benefits.
- Potential regressions.
- Security implications.
- Required tests.
- Rollback strategy.

Require explicit approval before applying a proposed integration to the
protected main project.

6. REVERSIBILITY

Every approved change must produce a change record.

Where Git is available, use commits, branches, and diffs.

Where Git is unavailable, use immutable snapshots and a transactional
change journal.

Provide a reliable rollback mechanism.

7. MODULARITY

Use clear interfaces between the frontend, backend, AI providers,
project analyzer, file processor, security engine, test runner,
integration engine, and reporting system.

Avoid monolithic architecture.

8. NO FAKE FUNCTIONALITY

Do not create buttons that pretend to perform operations.

Do not use hardcoded success messages for builds or tests.

Do not populate real-looking dashboards with fabricated project metrics.

If a feature cannot yet be implemented, mark it as unavailable or
not implemented and explain what is missing.

9. INCREMENTAL IMPLEMENTATION

Build and verify the application in small, coherent stages.

After each stage:
- Inspect the changed files.
- Run the available checks.
- Fix discovered regressions.
- Record the actual results.
- Continue only when required dependencies are ready.

10. RESOURCE AWARENESS

The application must work within its actual available compute, storage,
API quotas, execution time, and memory limits.

Do not assume unlimited tokens, unlimited file uploads, unlimited
cloud execution, or unrestricted network access.

