3. PROJECT INTAKE AND ZIP IMPORT ENGINE
==================================================

Implement a real ZIP upload and inspection workflow.

The user must be able to:
- Select a ZIP file from the device.
- Drag and drop a ZIP file on supported desktop browsers.
- View upload progress.
- Cancel an upload.
- Inspect archive metadata.
- Start a security scan.
- Preview the detected project structure.
- Approve creation of a working project.

Support configurable upload limits.

Never trust the filename, extension, MIME type, or metadata supplied
by the user.

Validate the archive using actual ZIP parsing and integrity checks.

Detect:
- Invalid ZIP signatures.
- Corrupted central directories.
- Truncated archives.
- Invalid compression data.
- Unsupported compression methods.
- Suspiciously high compression ratios.
- Excessive numbers of entries.
- Excessive uncompressed sizes.
- Path traversal attempts.
- Absolute file paths.
- Windows drive-letter paths.
- Symbolic-link abuse.
- Duplicate archive entries.
- Case-sensitive and case-insensitive path collisions.
- Unexpected nested archives.
- Unsupported file types.

Protect against ZIP bombs.

Enforce limits for:
- Maximum compressed upload size.
- Maximum total extracted size.
- Maximum individual file size.
- Maximum file count.
- Maximum extraction depth.
- Maximum processing time.
- Maximum memory consumption.

Reject or quarantine unsafe archives.

Never extract files directly into the application's executable directory.

Use a dedicated workspace with canonicalized paths.

Ensure that every extracted destination remains inside the authorized
workspace directory.

Do not follow symlinks during extraction.

Preserve the original archive unchanged.

After successful inspection, create a project intake report containing:

- Original filename.
- Original archive size.
- SHA-256.
- Number of archive entries.
- Number of valid files.
- Number of rejected entries.
- Total estimated extracted size.
- Detected project type.
- Detected frameworks.
- Detected languages.
- Build system.
- Package manifests.
- Available tests.
- Security findings.
- Extraction status.
- Unresolved uncertainties.

The report must reflect real processing results.

