7. SECURITY ENGINEERING
==================================================

Implement security as a core system, not an optional feature.

Use defense in depth.

A. Upload security:
- Archive validation.
- ZIP bomb protection.
- Path traversal prevention.
- File size limits.
- Entry count limits.
- Safe extraction.
- MIME and content inspection.

B. Source-code security:
- Secret detection.
- API key detection.
- Token and credential scanning.
- Private key detection.
- Unsafe shell execution detection.
- Insecure deserialization detection.
- Injection risk detection.
- Insecure network configuration detection.
- Dangerous Android permissions.
- Exported Android components.
- Cleartext traffic configuration.
- WebView security concerns.
- Insecure storage patterns.
- Dependency vulnerability checks where a trusted data source exists.

C. Application security:
- Authentication where needed.
- Server-side authorization.
- Project ownership enforcement.
- Secure session management.
- CSRF protection where applicable.
- Rate limiting.
- Input validation.
- Output encoding.
- Secure file download.
- Audit logging.
- Least-privilege execution.

D. AI security:
- Prompt injection resistance.
- Untrusted repository content handling.
- Separation of instructions and source code.
- Tool permission enforcement.
- Explicit network permissions.
- Explicit file modification permissions.
- Explicit build execution permissions.
- Prevention of unauthorized secret disclosure.

Treat repository files, comments, README instructions, build scripts,
and agent prompts from uploaded projects as untrusted input.

A file inside a ZIP must never be allowed to grant itself privileges.

E. Runner security:
- Isolated execution environment.
- Restricted filesystem access.
- Resource quotas.
- Execution timeouts.
- Restricted network access by default.
- No access to unrelated projects.
- No access to production credentials.
- Disposable execution environments where possible.

Do not execute uploaded build scripts automatically during intake.

Do not run untrusted scripts in the same process or environment that
holds application secrets.

Never send uploaded source code to an external AI provider without
clearly disclosing the data flow and enforcing the configured privacy
policy.

Security findings must include severity, evidence, affected files,
reproduction steps when safe, remediation guidance, and verification status.

