11. CONTROLLED PROJECT INTEGRATION
==================================================

This is one of the most important modules.

The user may provide:
- An existing NOVIN project.
- A second ZIP with additional features.
- Code generated by another AI.
- An isolated source file.
- A new module.
- A new agent.
- A bug fix.
- A replacement implementation.

Never merge an input archive directly into the main project.

Use the following workflow.

PHASE A: SOURCE REGISTRATION

Register the incoming artifact.

Calculate its hash.

Record its origin and upload time.

Preserve the original.

PHASE B: SOURCE ANALYSIS

Determine the actual file types, modules, APIs, dependencies,
configuration, and intended purpose.

PHASE C: BASELINE COMPARISON

Compare the incoming artifact with the current project.

Identify:
- New files.
- Existing files with identical content.
- Existing files with different content.
- Conflicting APIs.
- Duplicate functionality.
- Dependency conflicts.
- Package conflicts.
- Configuration conflicts.
- Missing dependencies.
- Potential security regressions.
- Required migrations.

PHASE D: INTEGRATION DESIGN

Determine the appropriate integration strategy.

Possible strategies:
- Add a new module.
- Extend an existing module.
- Replace an implementation.
- Adapt an API.
- Introduce an adapter.
- Add an interface.
- Migrate a data model.
- Update configuration.
- Reject the proposed integration.
- Request further information.

Prefer minimal, cohesive, reversible changes.

Do not create a new abstraction merely for appearance.

Do not introduce unnecessary frameworks or dependencies.

PHASE E: IMPACT ANALYSIS

Show the user:
- Files to add.
- Files to modify.
- Files that may be removed.
- Dependencies to add or update.
- APIs affected.
- Existing features at risk.
- Security implications.
- Tests required.
- Rollback strategy.

PHASE F: PREVIEW

Display a unified diff and a file-by-file explanation.

Provide a side-by-side comparison where appropriate.

Allow the user to inspect every proposed change.

PHASE G: APPROVAL

Require explicit user approval before modifying the protected main project.

PHASE H: SANDBOX INTEGRATION

Apply approved changes to an isolated branch or working copy.

PHASE I: VERIFICATION

Run available static analysis, tests, dependency checks, and builds.

Compare the result with the baseline.

PHASE J: ACCEPTANCE

Present the evidence and request final approval before promoting
the candidate into the protected main project.

PHASE K: RECORD

Save the accepted version, hashes, change records, test results,
and final engineering report.

If any mandatory gate fails, do not silently promote the candidate.

