==================================================
20. VERSION MANAGEMENT AND ROLLBACK
==================================================

Create a reliable project versioning system.

Each version should contain:
- Project ID.
- Version ID.
- Parent version.
- Source hash.
- Change set.
- Build configuration.
- Test summary.
- Security summary.
- Approval record.
- Creation timestamp.
- Artifact hashes where applicable.

Use semantic versioning where appropriate, supplemented by a unique
build or revision identifier.

Keep uploaded archives immutable.

Maintain a protected main branch or equivalent immutable baseline.

Perform changes in isolated candidate branches or snapshots.

Provide:
- Version comparison.
- File-level diff.
- Change history.
- Rollback preview.
- Approved rollback execution.
- Version integrity verification.

Never overwrite a known-good version without preserving a recoverable copy.

==================================================
21. DATA STORAGE AND PRIVACY
==================================================

Implement storage appropriate for a real multi-project application.

Use a database for structured metadata.

Use controlled object or filesystem storage for project archives,
source files, artifacts, and reports.

Do not use in-memory state as the only persistence mechanism.

Enforce project-level authorization on every backend operation.

Prevent one user or project from reading another project's files.

Use secure upload and download endpoints.

Avoid exposing internal filesystem paths.

Do not log API keys, passwords, tokens, signing secrets, or private keys.

Define configurable retention policies.

Provide project export and deletion workflows.

Clearly document which information is stored locally, remotely, and
sent to an AI provider.

If a persistent database or storage service is unavailable in the
current environment, implement a clearly labeled development mode
and do not pretend that it provides production-grade persistence.

