24. ERROR HANDLING AND RECOVERY
==================================================

Implement explicit recovery behavior for:

- Failed ZIP uploads.
- Interrupted uploads.
- Invalid archives.
- Extraction errors.
- Corrupted files.
- AI provider failures.
- Rate limits.
- Network interruptions.
- Database failures.
- Worker crashes.
- Build timeouts.
- Test failures.
- Dependency installation failures.
- Interrupted integrations.
- Failed rollbacks.

Use idempotent operations where practical.

Do not retry destructive operations blindly.

Do not repeat an integration if the previous operation may already
have modified the candidate workspace.

Use operation IDs and persistent execution records.

When a failure occurs, preserve diagnostic evidence and return the
workspace to a known safe state when possible.

==================================================
25. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
==================================================

The first release must not be declared complete merely because
the user interface renders.

Verify these requirements individually.

G0: Application starts in the actual target environment.

G1: The user can create and persist a project.

G2: A valid ZIP can be uploaded and inspected.

G3: An invalid ZIP is rejected safely.

G4: Archive path traversal is prevented.

G5: ZIP bomb limits are enforced.

G6: Original source archives remain unchanged.

G7: A real project inventory is generated.

G8: The project type is detected from actual evidence.

G9: Duplicate candidates are identified and explained.

G10: Security scanning produces evidence-backed findings.

G11: The AI can inspect authorized project context.

G12: A candidate patch can be created without modifying the protected
     source baseline.

G13: A real diff can be displayed.

G14: Unauthorized changes cannot be promoted.

G15: A supported test can be executed and its real result recorded.

G16: Build status reflects actual execution.

G17: Failed operations are visible and recoverable where possible.

G18: Project history survives application restart.

G19: Reports are generated from actual operation records.

G20: Rollback can be tested without destroying the baseline.

G21: Secrets do not appear in browser bundles or generated reports.

G22: Project-level access controls are tested.

G23: Core workflows are usable on Android-sized screens.

G24: The application has automated regression tests.

G25: All remaining limitations are documented.

For every gate, record:

- Status.
- Evidence.
- Test method.
- Result.
- Remaining limitation.

Allowed statuses:

PASS
PARTIAL
FAIL
BLOCKED
UNKNOWN
NOT TESTED
NOT VERIFIED

A gate may be marked PASS only when its acceptance condition is
actually demonstrated.

